"For $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb{N}$ with $\\alpha\\leq\\beta$ and $\\beta$ even, fix a set $\\mathcal U$ of $\\beta$ vectors in $\\mathbb{C}^{\\alpha}$ generically. A Latin Rectangle for $\\mathcal U$ is an $\\alpha\\times\\beta$ matrix whose entries are drawn from $\\mathcal U$, with no entry occurring more than once in any row or column. For a Latin Rectangle $L$, let $\\operatorname{coldet}(L)$ be the product over columns of the determinants formed from the column vectors. **Latin Rectangle Conjecture.** One has\n\n$$\n\\sum_L \\operatorname{coldet}(L)\\neq 0,\n$$\n\nwhere the sum ranges over all Latin Rectangles for $\\mathcal U$. This conjecture concerns the nonvanishing of an explicit sum of products of determinants for generic vectors; the source gives no evidence of resolution."